# Swift/iOS coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules to modern Swift
(6.x) and SwiftUI idiom. Applies only to `*.swift` files — everything not
Swift-specific still comes from the common rules this file `extends`.

## Naming and structure

- Types (`struct`, `class`, `enum`, `protocol`) are `UpperCamelCase`;
  properties, methods, and cases are `lowerCamelCase` — never
  `snake_case` for either.
- Protocol names read as a capability (`Fetchable`, `Cacheable`) or a
  role noun (`DataSource`, `Delegate`); do not suffix a protocol with
  `Protocol`.
- An identifier's name shrinks with its scope: a tight closure parameter
  can be `$0`/a one-letter name, a public API member gets a full
  descriptive name.
- File name matches the primary type it declares (`OrderService.swift`
  declares `OrderService`); one primary type per file for anything
  public.

## Value types vs reference types

- Default to `struct` (and `enum` for closed sets of cases) for model and
  state types — value semantics mean a copy cannot be mutated out from
  under another owner, which is what SwiftUI's data flow assumes.
- Reach for `class` only when you need reference semantics on purpose:
  shared mutable state across owners, identity that must be preserved
  (`===`), or Objective-C interop. Justify a `class` model type in review
  rather than defaulting to it out of habit from other languages.
- A `class` used as SwiftUI-observed state is marked `@Observable` (see
  `patterns.mdc`) and, when its mutable state is touched from view code,
  isolated to `@MainActor`.

## Guard for early exit

- Use `guard let`/`guard` with an early `return`/`continue`/`throw` to
  unwrap a precondition and keep the "happy path" unindented, rather than
  nesting the whole function body inside an `if let`.
- Prefer a single `guard` with multiple comma-separated conditions over a
  pyramid of nested `if let`s when several optionals must all be present
  before the function can proceed.

## Optionals

- Treat `!` (force-unwrap) and `try!` (force-try) as a last resort, not a
  convenience — reserve them for a value whose absence is a genuine
  programmer error that should crash in development (an `IBOutlet` wired
  in a storyboard, a compile-time-guaranteed non-nil literal), never for
  network responses, decoded JSON, user input, or anything else that can
  legitimately be nil or throw in production.
- Prefer `guard let`/`if let`, nil-coalescing (`??`) with a real default,
  or `try`/`try?` with explicit handling. Use `#require(...)` (Swift
  Testing) or `XCTUnwrap` in tests instead of `!` when a test needs to
  unwrap and fail with a clear message if the value is missing.
- Chain optional access with `?.`/optional chaining rather than
  force-unwrapping an intermediate step in a chain.

## @Observable vs legacy ObservableObject

- For code targeting iOS 17+, model types that drive SwiftUI view
  updates use the `@Observable` macro, not the legacy
  `ObservableObject`/`@Published` combination — `@Observable` tracks
  property-level access automatically, so a view only re-renders for the
  properties it actually reads, where `@Published` re-renders on any
  change to the whole object.
- Mark an `@Observable` class `@MainActor` unless the project has set a
  module-wide default actor isolation to `MainActor` — this keeps its
  mutable state confined to the main actor by construction (see
  `patterns.mdc` for the concurrency implications).
- Only reach for `ObservableObject`/`@Published`/`@StateObject`/
  `@ObservedObject` when the project's minimum deployment target
  predates iOS 17, or an existing codebase has not yet migrated; do not
  introduce the legacy pattern in new code on a project that already
  targets iOS 17+.

## Formatting and linting

- Format with the project's configured formatter (`swift-format` or
  `swiftformat`) before finishing a change; do not hand-format around a
  formatter that is already configured in the project.
- Fix a SwiftLint finding at its root cause; do not silence it with a
  blanket `// swiftlint:disable` covering more than the one line it
  actually applies to, and never disable a rule repository-wide to clear
  one finding.
